{"author":{"id":"6c7374a500bfd4cb191ae45b3f9d4b7a3d9d7f0324d04add217e8cbefeb7e1b9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792312279,"nonce":"AIxiZpyXYeYrePgQp1/6JYPxrDM=","title":"Chrome never finishes compiling the backdrop pipeline: the lens makes it too large","message":"In a page, the banner's first frame never finished: the page stopped answering, with Apple's MTLCompilerService at 96% CPU for as long as it ran and the GPU process idle.\n\n**Cause**\n\n`BackdropShader` is one shader for frosted and liquid glass. Its generated WGSL is 253 KB, because the generator inlines `boxDistance` at each of its five uses in the lens and the rim, and holds every local in a private global. Chrome compiled the page's other eight pipelines in under 160 ms each and this one not at all in 60 s. Timed alone in a test page: with the liquid code removed it is 72 KB and takes 2.6 s; with its blur loop removed as well, 30 ms. Compile time grows with the whole shader and the loop together.\n\n**Fix**\n\n`BackdropShader` is the frosted filter alone, and `LiquidBackdropShader` the whole of what it was, a pipeline of its own made when liquid glass is first drawn. The renderer picks per backdrop from `gradient.x`. The Glass scene renders byte for byte as before, natively.\n\n**Open**\n\nThe frosted pipeline still takes about 2.6 s to compile in Chrome on Metal, in the first frame that has glass. The blur loop is what makes it slow; a fixed number of taps, or the blur done in the rows pass only, would be tried next. The liquid pipeline itself should get the same treatment before a page can draw liquid glass.","files":null}]}